   104	  resolves (passed, 2026-11-09). (d) Doubling ground truth: the driver's doubling stage reproduces one registered
   105	  fresh-market D2+H3 doubling record under the draft-5 criterion, on the Mac. (c) Unit tests: the driver refuses a
   106	  changed reference hash, a missing input record, a wrong configuration hash, and an output directory inside `eval/all`.
   107	
   108	## 4. Diagnostics (evaluation only, no search)
   109	
   110	For three rules on seven markets: the base and doubled D2+H3 winners (42 policies) and the base D2 winners (21; the doubled
   111	D2 winners are not included), on the 200 evaluation seeds, re-run with reset and ban instrumentation (63 single-policy
   112	evaluations; the existing gain records carry no reset counts, so none is reused); the selected `x`; per episode the number of resets, the probability of at least one reset, the rounds of resets,
   113	and the rounds spent banned. Check that every D2 winner has zero resets. **Reverse switch (draft 5):** each of the 18 D2 winners re-evaluated with
   114	`x = 1` and every other knob unchanged (18 more evaluations). The diagnostics are built in the driver and run free on the
   115	Mac before the pod, under the draft-5 tolerance against a pod spot check of one cell. **Fixed-policy switch:** each of the 42 D2+H3
   116	winners re-evaluated with `x` set to 0 and every other knob unchanged (42 more; a winner that already has `x = 0` is
   117	reported as identical and not re-run); g(winner) - g(winner, x = 0) is the effect of the switch
   118	without re-optimisation, reported beside I.
   119	
   120	## 5. Analysis (fixed now)
   121	
   122	- **Intervals.** Per market: paired seed bootstrap (9,999 resamples, percentile, 95%), the same resampled seed indices for
   123	  every rule and family of that market, so contrasts across rules stay paired. Pooled: equal market weights; in each
   124	  replicate, seeds are resampled independently within each market and the pooled statistic is the mean of the six market
   125	  means; percentile interval. Nominal and descriptive; conditional on the six markets and the frozen policies and
   126	  references.
   127	- **Statistics.** I(r, m) and pooled I(r); the contrasts I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3); the absolute gaps
   128	  G(D2+H3) = g_B3 - g_R3 under D2+H3 and G(D2) under D2, pooled.
   129	- **Share.** S = 1 - pooled G(D2) / pooled G(D2+H3), a ratio of pooled contrasts, computed in every bootstrap replicate.
   130	  S is reported only if the lower bound of pooled G(D2+H3) exceeds 0.05; otherwise "not defined". No clipping; S can be
   131	  negative or above 1. The absolute gaps are always reported beside it.
   132	- **Market sensitivity (draft 5).** The sign count of I(B3) - I(R3) and I(C*) - I(R3) over the six markets, and S with
   133	  each market left out in turn (six values).
   134	- **Doubling flags.** For each D2 cell, the attack change and the reference-reselection change of its doubling check are
   135	  reported separately.
   136	- **Doubled versions** of the same statistics, as in the paper's `tab:ph-doubled`. The base (frozen) searches are primary,
   137	  as in the paper; the doubled versions are reported beside them.
   138	- No confirmatory test, no multiplicity family, no change to any registered decision.
   139	
   140	## 6. Pre-specified expectations and labels
   141	
   142	Written before any fresh D2 result exists. **The six markets, their seeds, and their D2+H3 outcomes were already observed;
   143	only the D2 outcomes are unseen.** This is a descriptive follow-up specified on 2026-11-09 after the round-9 review and
   144	after the headline contrast was seen, not an independent replication.
   145	
   146	Expectations: on all six fresh markets (each market counted; a single market against it fails the expectation), I(B3) > I(R3) and I(C*) > I(R3); pooled S defined and greater than 0.5. Expectations are
   147	scored on point estimates (this is a descriptive follow-up; "met" does not mean that an interval excludes the threshold),
   148	with the denominator gate of section 5 applied separately to the base and the doubled results. Each expectation is
   149	scored as met, failed, or not established: **met** if it holds under both the base and the doubled searches; **failed** if
   150	it fails under both; **not established (search-sensitive)** if the two disagree. An undefined S and a missing cell are
   151	reported as such, separately from failures. A failure is not read as evidence for any particular cause (search error and
   152	mixed outcomes are possible). Only if the pooled-S expectation is met may the paper say: within the D2 family, at the
   153	fixed parameters and search budgets, most of the estimated B3 - R3 gap under the prescribed searches disappears when the
   154	attacker cannot replace its identity.
   155	
   156	## 7. Cost
   157	
   158	**Work.** Measured benchmark (`experiments/v2/benchmark/timings.json`): one unit = a headline D2+H3 cell, 0.579
   159	core-hours. Per D2 cell: 1 unit + 160 further seeds (0.046) + doubling with held-out evaluation (0.479); 18 cells = 27.45
   160	units. Diagnostics: 63 instrumented evaluations + up to 42 switch evaluations = up to 105 single-policy evaluations of
   161	200 episodes, priced conservatively at the profit-evaluation stage's cost for 200 seeds (0.058 units each, although that
   162	stage evaluates more than one profile) = about 6.1 units. Total about 33.6 units = **about 19.4 core-hours**. Draft 5
   163	moves the diagnostics to the Mac (free) and adds the pod gate (about 0.34 USD); the pod estimate below is kept as an
   164	upper bound.
   165	
   166	**Billed pod time.** One 16-vCPU pod at the recorded 0.56 USD per hour; searches with 16 workers, evaluation, doubling,
   167	and diagnostics with at most 10 (operations ledger). Stage schedule with incomplete worker batches and equal benchmark
   168	task times: searches, evaluation, and doubling about 1.77 hours (internal review's computation); diagnostics about 0.6
   169	hours (105 evaluations in batches of 10); preparation 0.3 hours; idle tail 0.3 hours = **about 3.0 billed hours, about
   170	1.66 USD**. The benchmark's unit price (0.0625 USD per unit, at its 0.108 USD per core-hour) gives about 2.10 USD; the plan
   171	uses the larger.
   172	
   173	| Item | Estimate | Cap |
   174	|---|---:|---:|
   175	| Option A: 18 D2 cells, doubling checks, diagnostics | 1.66 to 2.10 USD | **10.00 USD** (author, 2026-11-09) |
   176	
   177	A failed attempt counts toward the cap; the cap leaves room for one early failure, not for a full second run. Option B (an X7-SC variant with replacement disabled) is dropped from this plan: its
   178	constrained family, incumbents, and corner controls would need their own specification (the frozen X7-SC search inserts
   179	D2+H3 incumbents that can replace), so it is not offered for approval.
   180	
   181	**Budget.** `docs/revision/budget-ledger.json`: spent 50.869 USD of the 55 USD approval limit. The author approved option A
   182	on the board on 2026-11-09 and set the cap to 10 USD ("make the cap $10 to ensure we're not blocked by a low cap"). The
   183	ledger needs a new category `followup` with cap 10.00 and the approval limit raised to 61 USD (50.869 + 10 = 60.869,
   184	`python3 -c "print(50.869+10)"`). `scripts/budget_v2.py` is frozen, so the tested wrapper
   185	`docs/revision/followup/budget_followup.py --approve 61 10 TEXT` records both, refuses a limit below spend plus the cap,
   186	and keeps the earlier approval text. The estimate is unchanged (1.66 to 2.10 USD); the higher cap only prevents a stop from a low
   187	cap. Stop at the cap.
   188	
   189	## 8. Steps
   190	
   191	1. Internal review of this plan to consensus; freeze it (commit).
   192	2. Decision card: approved on 2026-11-09, cap 10 USD (card `touchstone-reset-spend`).
   193	3. On approval: write and test the driver (TDD), run validation (a) and (b) on the Mac (free); then one pod for the 18
   194	   cells, deleted and verified after the run.
   195	4. Analysis: a new `reset` block in `posthoc_v2.py` (tested first), a table, and a paragraph in section 9 with sites; the
   196	   section-1 headline numbers enter at the same time, labelled exploratory.
