## User preferences

- When an application draft is “too generic,” use specific evidence for why this institution, why the work, and why now; avoid claims such as “frontier of machine intelligence” unless a concrete fact earns them. [Task 1]
- “use american english and remove-ai-slop and simplify the language” -> write in plain, direct, human American English; retain precise qualifiers rather than polished application clichés. [Task 1]
- Frame a disproved original claim constructively but honestly: “Testing showed that my original claim did not hold. I published the findings and used them to refine the research question.” [Task 1]

## Reusable knowledge

- Touchstone is evidence of empirical research discipline about checking work when only a sample can be checked; it is not itself a demonstrated general AI-safety or AI-control result. [Task 1]
- The sharp proposal question is “Which work should we check, and when, if agents know how we monitor them?” Proposed work compares random auditing with provider-history/time-since-joining selection at a fixed budget; measures undetected misconduct, attacker gains, and cost to honest providers; and keeps timing separate from selection. [Task 1]
- Verify current policy claims at their primary source. RSP v3.4 (effective 2026-07-08) supported the limited point that Anthropic could not give highly specific advance detail on evaluations determining whether risk thresholds were passed. [Task 1]
- Keep application/CV numbers externally aligned: a local 955-cycle kernel passed official tests and 100 random seeds, but the CV must remain at the leaderboard-confirmed 964 cycles (#39/579 at the rollout) until the leaderboard changes. [Task 1]

## Failures and how to do differently

- Generic motivation -> replace abstractions with the verified RSP point, Touchstone, the career transition, and the concrete audit research direction; do not cite third-party policy summaries. [Task 1]
- An earlier Claude-attacker experiment was inconclusive because its action vocabulary could not express the audit rule -> preserve the higher-level lesson that agents need actions capable of exploring strategies around rules, without overexplaining the implementation. [Task 1]
- Do not upgrade proposed LLM-agent research or a locally measured benchmark into an established AI-control finding or an externally confirmed CV result. [Task 1]
